WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:03.219
"The Batman's" played a joke on all of us.

00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:08.090
"I'm just here to unmask the truth about this
cesspool we call a city."

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.670
"The Batman" offers significant changes to
characters general audiences thought they

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.670
knew well.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.950
All you have to do is take a look at the Caped
Crusader himself, played this time around

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:16.990
by Robert Pattinson.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:20.680
While he's the World's Greatest Detective
in the comics, that side of him had never

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.030
really been explored in a live-action film
before.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:27.090
That all changes in "The Batman" as he's on
the hunt for a mysterious serial killer going

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.199
by the name of Riddler.

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:32.800
He has to solve the clues, and to further
add to the detective aesthetic, Batman speaks

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.989
in voiceover like an old-school noir film.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.110
Of course, some things are destined to remain
the same forever, such as Batman's eternal

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.480
struggle with the Joker.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.110
In virtually every iteration of the character,
the Caped Crusader has to tussle with the

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.239
Clown Prince of Crime, and lo and behold,
the Joker does make a brief appearance in

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:49.239
"The Batman."

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:52.920
After the Riddler's safely in custody, he
speaks with his next-door cellmate, and while

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.360
he never mentions a name, it's fairly obvious
this character, played by Barry Keoghan, is

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.130
supposed to be the newest version of the Joker.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:04.500
"Getting to be that, it's like – words can't
describe it."

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.229
For years, all of DC's cinematic efforts came
to exist under the banner of the DC Extended

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:10.229
Universe.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:13.229
Similar to the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
this meant that all of the characters existed

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:17.140
under one roof, so Batman could appear in
a Superman movie and vice versa.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.420
However, unlike the MCU, this venture has
become stilted in recent years as the massive

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.820
team-up film "Justice League" failed to ignite
a franchise.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.969
Since that time, some movies have kept the
series going, like "Birds of Prey."

00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:33.549
However, other DC projects have come to exist
in their own continuity, such as 2019's "Joker."

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:37.359
Prior to its release, there was confirmation
that "The Batman" would exist in its own separate

00:01:37.359 --> 00:01:38.359
continuity.

00:01:38.359 --> 00:01:42.700
However, there were questions if it would
connect with any other DC efforts, particularly

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.389
"Joker" where Joaquin Phoenix portrayed the
famed villain.

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:49.319
With the above-mentioned scene in "The Batman,"
we now know once and for all that "The Batman"

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:51.649
won't connect to anything that's come before.

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:54.429
By all appearances, a new Joker is in town.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:57.369
However, that doesn't mean "The Batman" will
exist in a vacuum.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:00.049
It's already been confirmed that two spinoffs
are in the works on HBO Max.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:04.670
The first will center on the Gotham Police
Department while the other focuses on Colin

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:05.880
Farrell's Penguin.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.709
There's even a possibility that if "The Batman
2" filmmakers don't want to use Joker as the

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:13.230
villain in that movie, he could pop up in
one of these shows to wreak havoc.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.500
It would seem "The Batman" is forging a new
path ahead for DC projects, and while he may

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.850
be the Dark Knight, the future couldn't be
brighter.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.650
"Don't throw your life away."

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.530
"Don't worry, honey – I've got nine of 'em."

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.230
With DC tampering with the multiverse in the
upcoming "Flash" movie, it's clear that while

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:33.180
some films will exist within the DCEU, others
will exist separately in their own universes.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.520
Of course, as Marvel has already shown us
with "Spider-Man: No Way Home," the multiverse

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.950
is a delicate concept, and it can easily break
to the point where other realities merge with

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:42.950
others.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.819
As of right now, three live-action Jokers
have appeared in DC theatrical films as of

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:47.819
late.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:51.709
In addition to Barry Keoghan's cameo in "The
Batman," there's also Joaquin Phoenix's Oscar-winning

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:54.109
portrayal as the Clown Prince of Crime in
"Joker."

00:02:54.109 --> 00:02:57.940
Jared Leto also recently revived his version
of the character for "Zack Snyder's Justice

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.519
League" after playing him for 2016's "Suicide
Squad."

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:05.499
That makes for three live-action Jokers out
there, not even getting into all of the cartoons

00:03:05.499 --> 00:03:08.269
and video games that have featured him in
recent memory.

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:12.120
It's an intriguing development, especially
seeing how DC Comics has introduced the concept

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.200
of three Jokers in the mainline DC continuity.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.109
In the limited series, "Batman: Three Jokers,"
Batman realizes that different versions of

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:20.730
Joker are out there.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.599
Not only does it make for a thrilling adventure,
but it also serves as a meta-commentary on

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:27.359
the purpose and function of the villain in
these stories.

00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:30.639
Could a live-action version of this story
materialize with the three Jokers we have

00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:34.150
now uniting for one wild and crazy escapade?

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.790
It's a long shot, but wilder things have happened
in the world of comic book movies.

